{%- comment -%}
  One product card, on its own, for the Recently Viewed strip to fetch.

  app.js requests /products/<handle>?section_id=recently-viewed-card and gets
  back exactly the markup snippets/product-card.liquid produces for that
  product — same badges, same wishlist button, same Quick Add, same GA4
  attributes, same money formatting.

  ── Why a fetch per card rather than rendering from JSON ─────────────────

  The obvious alternative is to keep a snapshot of each product in
  localStorage — title, image, price — and build the card in JavaScript.
  Two things are wrong with that.

  The price would be whatever it was when the shopper last looked. A strip
  quoting a price the cart then disagrees with is the bait-and-switch
  pattern the compliance section names, and it would be introduced by the
  one feature that exists to re-sell something they already liked.

  And the card markup would exist twice. The theme already carries a second,
  older card renderer in app.js from the static build; it predates the GA4
  attributes, so anything drawn with it is invisible to analytics. Adding a
  third copy guarantees the three drift.

  So only handles are stored, and the card is asked for fresh. Four small
  requests, each cacheable by the browser, and the price is always the one
  Shopify will charge.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if product != blank -%}{% render 'product-card', product: product %}{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{ "name": "Recently viewed card", "settings": [] }
{% endschema %}
